title The box counted and never read, and the instrument built twice
prompt The baton -- open the round, read the rota row, take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, reds first.
rota lap 4039, N mod 5 = 4 -- Earth, already read on this tree at 06:38 today, so advanced by hand to row 0 and then to row 1, Air, the row that feels: law and boundary, and the sense is touch, which only works at a surface. Its fixed seat says you check whether a part stands free BY TRYING TO PULL IT OUT, and both of this lap's findings came from exactly that -- a hand run along the fence line of one claim, finding one post firm to the eye and loose to the hand.
think Opened with fleet_round_open, clean on 702ede1e8f, and its own report carried the whole lap: stashed_entries=2, one of them stamped 20260906-100506, six seconds before I read the clock. My own round-open had just parked a finished lap.
think Read the stash before reading anything else. stash@{0} held fifteen files -- a repaired scan, its control, its witness, a new Mantra guard, a pen control, two ledger rows on their own shelf, a corrected page, and a complete session log at 20260906.094442. A whole round, one commit short.
think Ran the cold roster anyway and held still for it, because the tree I stand on is the tree before the restore. 135 guards, 130 green, 1 red, 4 gated, 866s, tree_moved=no. The single red was standing_equipment -- and the repair for it was in the box the entire pass. That sentence is the lap.
think Applied the stash and resolved two conflicts by hand: the fold recital wanted both a peer's %454 row and mine, in stamp order; the card's Landed table wants exactly one row. The index row came back third because the merge placed it where the stash had written it, under two peer rows that landed after -- moved to the top, since the shelf reads newest first.
think Verified rather than trusted. The repaired scan reads runs_red=0, runs_self_red=1, self_red named, verdict=ok, which closes the cold pass's one red. standing_equipment_witness GREEN with its control's twenty-plus behaviors including two anti-bypass phases. mantra_a1_equality_witness GREEN on metal, four desks built and run, six pen breaks.
think Then took %464's own booked remainder, since it names the instrument in words: report, at the open, any stash holding a session-logs path absent from the tree. Wrote it as its own scan, control and witness rather than widening standing_equipment_scan -- that scan answers is the roster honest, and a second subject in it would be a braid rather than a part that stands free.
think Two design calls, and both were forced by measurement rather than taste. LANDED HAD TO INCLUDE THE INDEX: reading HEAD alone, the guard reds its own hot pass, because a restored record is staged between git add and the commit the lap ends at. The state that admits -- staged and never committed -- is already refused one guard over by staged_uncommitted and run_verdict=lap_unclosed, so one reading, one home. And LANDED HAD TO RESOLVE BY BASENAME, or the guard reds the day any room folds, which is a guard that reds on ordinary work.
think Bounded the walk at 16 to match the runner's own max_stash_entries, and made a box past the bound REFUSE rather than pass. A stash it never opened may hold a parked record, so answering ok would be %463's fault one level up: a census reporting the room empty because it stopped at the door.
think The control failed on its first run after four green lines, and the cause is worth keeping. set -e plus a command substitution assigned to a variable carries the scan's non-zero exit outward, so the script died at its first SUCCESSFUL refusal -- and the output read exactly like a passing control that had run out of phases. Both helpers carry set +e now, and the reason is written beside them.
think Fetched before booking and the spine had moved: %472 and %473 were spent upstream on 20260906.070240 and 20260906.090312 while my stash sat parked. reds_spine_derive_scan named the repair -- squatting %472 ... derives above %476 -- and the pair renumbered to %477/%478 across ten files with their stamps unmoved.
obs THE REPAIR FOR THE ONE RED WAS IN THE BOX DURING THE PASS THAT REPORTED IT. That is the whole finding, and it is REDS %467's sentence in the instrument that watches the fleet: presence is the cheaper question wearing the expensive one's answer. stashed_entries=2 was on screen for 866 seconds. A count of fifteen files reads the same whether a stash duplicates what landed or holds a whole round nobody received, and only the second is a hole in the one channel this organization keeps.
obs A STASH IS ORDINARY; A RECORD NOBODY RECEIVED IS NOT. The runner's own header argues that its stash reading must never gate, and it is right -- about stashes. The claim I gate on is narrower: a lap's ACCOUNT absent from session-logs. Those two sentences part, and naming where they part is what earns a gate beside a reading that correctly refuses to be one.
obs THE GUARD FOUND TWO PARKED RECORDS ON ITS FIRST RUN, which is better evidence than a zero would have been. One was this seat's own finished lap; the other a census from 01:38 whose code a peer's twin superseded as %463 -- two ships building the same instrument the same night, agreeing to the file on 1,713 bodies. Both records restored byte-for-byte, SHA-256 verified against the stashed blobs, so the box now holds nothing the channel lacks and the guard reads zero for a reason rather than by luck.
obs I HIT THE SAME SHAPE MYSELF, IN A THROWAWAY WAITER. A loop written as while pgrep -f 'sh tools/fixtures/s/standing_equipment_run' matched its own bash -c command line, so it could never exit -- an instrument whose subject includes itself, which is %477's exact fault in a five-line shell loop. Matching by PID fixed it. Worth recording because it took me three checks to see, on the same morning I was reading the row that names it.
obs THE REBASE FOUND THE ROW'S TWIN ALREADY LANDED, AND THAT IS %464'S SHARPEST EVIDENCE. My stash held the roster-latch repair from 09:44; a peer had landed the identical diagnosis and the identical fix as %475 at 07:02, from a tree that had it while my copy sat parked. Same self-row exclusion, same both-ways proof. I took upstream's whole -- theirs is shared and mine was not -- and withdrew my half of the shelf. Half a finished lap thrown away is the exact cost that row exists to name, and it was paid on the round that closed it.
obs AND THE LIVING CARD ITSELF STOOD PUBLISHED WITH CONFLICT MARKERS. The rebase conflicted against `<<<<<<< HEAD` and `>>>>>>> 8b22606c4` that were already committed on xy/main, around the card's own Landed row, through three commits. Swept tree-wide: exactly one file, and it was that one. Nothing in the tree looks, and the reason is structural -- every instrument reads a file for a different property, and living_pin_max_bytes, the one meter aimed squarely at that file, reads LENGTH. A marker makes a pin longer, so the damage read as ordinary growth. Booked %481 and closed on conflict_marker_witness, tier lap.
obs THE TIER WAS BOUGHT BY MEASURING RATHER THAN GUESSING. The first draft walked 15,681 tracked files one grep at a time: 61 seconds, which is a cadence guard, and a cadence guard would let a marker stand published for four laps. One git grep with pathspec exclusions reads the same corpus in 0.3s. The rewrite also fixed a real blindness -- git ls-files lists an unmerged path once per stage, so a tree standing mid-merge reported the same file three times, which is precisely the tree this guard exists for.
obs I TYPED A NUMBER THAT MEASUREMENT FALSIFIED, IN THE LAP ABOUT THAT EXACT CLASS. I wrote "22 readings over 10 planted states in 8 real repositories" for the marker control in four places; counting new_repo, run_scan and the printed key=value lines answers 23, 10 and 9. Corrected at all four sites before the send. The card's own spec asks for a writer beside each guard, and this is the third kind of number that wants one.
obs AND THEN THE REBASE WITHDREW HALF MY ROUND, WHICH IS THE FINDING RATHER THAN A SETBACK. Two of this lap's three findings had already been landed by peers while my copies sat in the stash: the roster-latch repair as %475 at 07:02, and stash_record itself -- the very instrument %464 asked for -- as %479 at 11:05, from another ship's tree, same diagnosis, same name, same night. I took both of theirs whole, since theirs are shared and mine were not, and withdrew my duplicates rather than shelving the same finding twice. THE INSTRUMENT BUILT TO REPORT A DUPLICATED LAP WAS ITSELF ONE OF THE DUPLICATES. That sentence is the round.
obs WHAT SURVIVES IS WHAT NOBODY ELSE HAD, and it is worth naming plainly rather than softening: conflict_marker, because the card's own published damage had no reader; mantra_a1_equality with the src/gate/README correction, because seven of eight proofs had no runner; and the two restored records, which are exactly what the peer's own new guard demands and which no other tree carries. The peer's guard reads them landed the moment this commit lands.
obs THE PEER'S RECOVERY AND MINE DIFFER IN ONE MECHANIC WORTH KEEPING. Theirs used git checkout <ref> -- <path>, which reads the TREE object and preserves mode 100755; I used git show <ref>:<path> >, which reads the blob and lands 100644 (%292). Both of my restored files are session logs at 100644 either way, so nothing was lost here -- yet the habit is theirs and I am adopting it.
obs THE FINAL PASS READ 142 GUARDS, 137 GREEN, ONE RED, AND THE RED WAS A LAGGING READING. standing_equipment reports the PREVIOUS pass's card, so it named the three reds this round had just repaired -- index_row_bound, remember_git_nib, reds_pin_capacity, all three green now. %475 fixed the self-latch; the one-pass lag for PEERS' reds remains by design, since the card is a record of what happened rather than of what is.
obs A NUMBER BOOKED FROM A PARKED STASH IS INVISIBLE TO --next TWICE OVER -- once for being local, once for being stashed. That is %464's own erratum firing a fifth time on the round that closed it, and it is fresh evidence for Keaton's standing question about whether an OPEN row should carry a claim, a seat and a stamp at START.
obs THE CARD COST MORE THAN THE CODE. The pin sat 236 bytes under its ceiling and my block ran 313 over that, so five passes of condensing went into my own paragraphs. I condensed a peer's %450 paragraph once, caught it, and put it back verbatim -- the card's own habit is to move nothing that is not mine, and a byte ceiling is exactly the pressure that makes a hand forget it.
loom witness=conflict_marker wall_s=3 scan_wall_s=0.3 scan_wall_s_perfile=61 files_scanned=15681 marked_files=0 readings=23 states=10 pens=9
loom final_pass=standing_equipment_run guards=142 green=137 red=1 gated=4 wall_s=705 tree_moved=no
